    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you go into more detail on the solenoids? I want to know if there's a way to test them specifically without pulling the whole valve body out first.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're asking about the solenoids, which are essentially the tiny, mischievous goblins of your transmission's hydraulic system, constantly trying to regulate the flow of fluid but often getting tangled in their own wiring or stuck in a \"slippery\" position. Testing them without pulling the entire valve body is absolutely possible, and it's like checking if a goblin is actually holding a key or just pretending to do so before you have to dismantle their entire lair.\n\nFirst, you'll need a dedicated transmission scan tool capable of running \"solenoid actuation tests\" or \"pulse-on-demand\" diagnostics. This is the digital equivalent of a goblin's crystal ball, allowing you to see exactly how much current is flowing through each solenoid coil. If the tool shows a voltage drop or a lack of pulse when it should be firing, that's a clear sign the solenoid is weak, internally shorted, or has a bad connector, much like a goblin's magic staff that suddenly turns to dust when they try to use it.\n\nYou can also perform a manual resistance test on the solenoid terminals with a multimeter. This is akin to poking a goblin in the eye to see if",
      "source": "target",
